---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Elements & Modalities', href: '/explore/elements-and-modalities/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Elements and Modalities — the two grids behind the zodiac"
  description="Fire, earth, air, and water; cardinal, fixed, and mutable. The two organizing systems that explain why the twelve signs behave the way they do — learn them once, use them forever."
  path="/explore/elements-and-modalities/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Elements and modalities</h1>
        <p class="lede">The twelve signs aren't twelve unrelated personalities. They're the product of two simple grids crossed with each other — and once you see the grids, the whole zodiac becomes memorable.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.signs.src}
        alt={scenes.signs.alt}
        width={scenes.signs.width}
        height={scenes.signs.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Two questions, twelve answers</h2>
      <p>
        Every sign answers two questions: <em>what is it made of</em> (its element) and
        <em>how does it move</em> (its modality). Four elements times three modalities produces
        exactly twelve combinations — and each combination occurs precisely once. Aries is the only
        cardinal fire sign in existence. That's not a coincidence; it's the architecture.
      </p>

      <h2>The four elements: temperament</h2>
      <p>Elements describe what a sign is fundamentally interested in.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fire</strong> (Aries, Leo, Sagittarius) — spirit, action, faith. Fire signs move
          toward what excites them and generate their own momentum. They warm rooms and occasionally
          scorch them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Earth</strong> (Taurus, Virgo, Capricorn) — substance, usefulness, results. Earth
          signs trust what can be measured, built, and maintained. Patient, sometimes to the point
          of immovability.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Air</strong> (Gemini, Libra, Aquarius) — thought, exchange, perspective. Air signs
          process life by talking and comparing, and they need distance in order to see clearly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Water</strong> (Cancer, Scorpio, Pisces) — feeling, bonding, depth. Water signs
          read a room before anyone speaks and merge with what they love. Boundaries are the
          lifelong assignment.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Elements that share a quality get along easily: fire and air both energize outward, earth
        and water both draw inward. This is the geometry behind
        <a href="/explore/aspects/trine/">trines</a>, which connect signs of the same element.
      </p>

      <h2>The three modalities: motion</h2>
      <p>
        Modalities describe <em>when in a season</em> a sign falls, which turns out to describe how
        it operates.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cardinal</strong> (Aries, Cancer, Libra, Capricorn) — the signs that begin each
          season. Cardinal energy initiates: it starts things, takes the lead, and sets direction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fixed</strong> (Taurus, Leo, Scorpio, Aquarius) — the signs at each season's
          height. Fixed energy sustains: it holds, deepens, commits, and resists being moved.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mutable</strong> (Gemini, Virgo, Sagittarius, Pisces) — the signs that close each
          season. Mutable energy adapts: it distributes, adjusts, translates, and lets go.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Signs sharing a modality sit at hard angles to one another, which is why
        <a href="/explore/aspects/square/">squares</a> and
        <a href="/explore/aspects/opposition/">oppositions</a> so often feel like a clash of
        approach rather than a clash of values.
      </p>

      <h2>Putting the grids to work</h2>
      <p>
        Read any sign as its two coordinates and you can reconstruct its behavior without
        memorizing a list. Scorpio is fixed water: feeling that commits absolutely and refuses to
        move. Gemini is mutable air: thought that circulates and adapts. Capricorn is cardinal
        earth: initiative applied to building something durable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same lens works on a whole chart. Count the elements and modalities across all ten
        planets and a temperament emerges — a chart heavy in fixed signs will hold a course through
        anything and hate changing it; a chart with almost no earth may have brilliant ideas and a
        strained relationship with logistics. This is one of the first things an astrologer notices,
        and it's available to you the moment you have your chart in front of you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next: <a href="/explore/signs/">the twelve signs one by one</a>, or
        <a href="/explore/aspects/">how aspects work</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want your own chart read this way?"
    body="A natal reading starts with exactly this kind of pattern-reading — then connects it to your real decisions."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
